<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Tundama</h1><p class="st-meta">1300 CE – 1539 CE (approx.) · capital Tundama</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Muisca lordship around modern Duitama whose ruler led the last organised resistance of the Muisca against the Spanish.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Tundama was a territory of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/muisca-confederation">Muisca Confederation</a> in the north of the high plateau, around modern Duitama in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boyaca-department">Boyacá</a>. It was ruled by a chief also called Tundama and stood among the lordships independent of the zaque of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/zacazgo-of-hunza">Zacazgo of Hunza</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Tundama was a chiefdom of farming communities growing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">potatoes</a> and quinoa around lakes and marshes. Its chiefs allied with neighbouring <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/iraca">Iraca</a> and fought both the zaque and the zipa. Little is known of its internal organisation beyond Spanish accounts of the conquest.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1539 the Spanish under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltasar-maldonado">Baltasar Maldonado</a> defeated Tundama at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-bonza">Battle of Bonza</a> after fierce resistance, and the chief was later killed. Duitama became a town in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/new-kingdom-of-granada">New Kingdom of Granada</a>, and the chief is remembered as a hero of indigenous resistance.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/tundama.jpg" alt="Tundama" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Petruss · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Duitama_Sirata.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1300 CE – 1539 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tundama</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Tundama</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Muisca religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Muisca (Chibcha)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/colombia">Colombia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Duitama</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1420&amp;focus=tundama" aria-label="Tundama on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2650 2044 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Tundama lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2942 2257h0M4910 989h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2970 2300h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M2970 2300h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Colombia. <a href="/atlas?year=1420&amp;focus=tundama">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

